The chance that a fair coin will come up heads 100 times is in row is 1 in
a bit over 1,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000. The chance of a
random coin having two heads is far higher than this (as coins have been
officially minted which accidentally had two heads, not to mention
relatively common fakes, and not nearly that many coins have been
minted). Weighted coins are extremely common (in fact, some standard
U.S. currency isn't fair), although a further factor would be necessary to
make it reasonable that tails never came up. 

In order to determine the correct odds for the last toss, you must
determine the odds of various possible situations given the previous
sequence of tosses, and then determine the odds of each outcome is each
situation. After 100 heads, it's virtually certain that you are in a
situation that always produces heads, and thus it is virtually certain
that the next toss will also be heads unless you change something.

The highest probability (particularly if you look at the coin) is that the
coin tosser happens to be sufficiently skilled at misdirection that the
outcome is determined not by chance but by tricks and distraction.

In fact, the RNG is likely to kill off this character in just this manner.

